Z||000000||80's BOY||04-05-2000||01:21 PM||||can anyone give me details about other fuel pumps or injectors that will help my 3.1 stroker get the fuel it deserves?? maybe some Firebird or Camaro parts would fit. need details on wiring. if so, what would be the expected h.p. increase from larger injectors/fuel pump or regulator?? explanations please!!||||reg||4
Z||000001||1FST2M6||04-05-2000||01:38 PM||||I'm using an 87-88 IROC fuel pump with way oversized RC Engineering injectors. The stock intectors from a 305 (Camaro/Fierbird) will drop right in..  you can get a less-costly fuel pump and it's just as good as the IROC one from The Fiero Store  <A HREF="http://www.leaderind.com" TARGET=_blank>www.leaderind.com</A>  thier Holley 255lphr unit ill support 300+ hp.. are you sure your running lean? not just having timing, or some other issue? You can also get an adjustable fuel pressure regulator from them. Stillen, NOS, Vortech, JUN, HKS, and Greddy all make in line fuel pressure regulators that can be hooked to your car. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||04-05-2000||02:48 PM||||Why do you think you need more fuel?||||reg||1
Z||000003||80's BOY||04-05-2000||04:00 PM||||it's the only thing i haven't modified on my 2.8 turned 3.1. i have an air/fuel gage coming from autometer so i can be sure, but my pressure is only about 34-40 at the rail on my pressure gage. with 0.060 overbore, three angle valve job, port/polished/milled heads and a freeflow exhaust among MANY other mods on my 3.1 stroker, my guess is it's time to toss the stock fuel system and give her some go-go!! just feeling a little under performanced considering all the mods. ||||reg||10
Z||000004||1FST2M6||04-05-2000||04:43 PM||||a set of the Tomoco units from The Fiero Store would probly do fine.. thier able to support an additional 75hp. They will not make that much HP but they can support it. are you close to a dyno? <A HREF="http://www.dynojet.com" TARGET=_blank>www.dynojet.com</A>  go and have them look at your car and run it up a few times. the tuner may have a better idea than just throwing a new fuel delivery system at it. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||04-05-2000||05:39 PM||||That's the problem. The first thought is to add fuel. What you need is a way to *verify* you need more fuel. Short of an expensive linear O2, the AFR gauge is a start. <P>A scanner is a bit easier to work with. If the O2 (regardless of it's limited and non-linear accuracy) is reading under 800-850mv on three different O2's, then I would add fuel, but if all three read above that, I don't think more is needed. Certainly, if you get into the upper 900mV range, less fuel is needed. You do it with three O2's and use the one that reads the lowest to keep from getting too lean and burning valves and pistons.<P>Certainly, I would get the fuel pressure back up to the 40-45 PSI range.<P>GM has to do a few little tricks. First, they have to limit the unburnt fuel on a throttle snap, so they limit the acceleration enrichment (pump shot). Second they have to keep the cylinders cool so they run WOT rich. <P>When you add fuel with bigger injectors, you increase the AE which gives you that initial seat of the pants acceleration, but as that decays out (in less than a second), the power enrichment (WOT) AFR is too much and the car is actually slower than it was before. Seat of the pants fools a person quickly.<P>I know some people don't like to hear it, but the best tuning tools are a stopwatch, an O2 meter and a friend to use them. Then you make changes.<P>IMHO||||reg||1
